 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d015: excitatory knot (k=7)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 2.6 and radius 1.64 with 7 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.34) and a 4-dim charge; 7 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 17 couplings (12% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 0.99 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 0.84 bonds per particle, largest molecule 5.5 particles, 12.0 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.122 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.116  random bonds 0.117\n  xor      0.603 vs ESN 0.514\u00b10.003  [beat]  no bonds 0.474  random bonds 0.501\n  narma10  0.357 vs ESN 0.844\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.349  random bonds 0.345\n  latch    0.873 vs ESN 0.678\u00b10.003  [beat]  no bonds 0.858  random bonds 0.868\n  mnist14  0.528 vs ESN 0.830\u00b10.007  [below]  no bonds 0.502  random bonds 0.539\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly matters on xor: the bonds its copies chose beat both no bonds and random bonds.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
